# Chapter 9 — The Relationships Behind the Machine

*The machine can perform the work; it cannot tell us whose claim the work must satisfy.*

Imagine a factory sold while its equipment remains in place. The motors have the same windings. The tools have the same dimensions. The building casts the same shadow. Yet after the transfer, different people may authorize production, receive revenue, exclude others, or decide to close the gates.

What changed?

A purely mechanical description cannot answer. Something happened through documents, payments, recognized authority, and the willingness or obligation of others to act accordingly. These events have physical embodiments and consequences, but their significance depends on relationships that no inspection of a motor can reveal.

Property is real without being a hidden chemical ingredient of the object owned.

This is a necessary starting point for historical materialism. Materialism does not require pretending that only things one can hold in a hand have causal power. A debt can alter a person's conduct. A legal judgment can determine who enters a building. A wage contract can organize the next month of someone's life. The question is how these relations are produced, sustained, and made effective through the activities of embodied people.

Marx's account of commodity fetishism asks how relations among producers take the form of relations among the products of their labor. Commodities have useful physical properties, but their exchange relations belong to a social organization of production. Those relations can confront participants as if they were independent powers of things.[1]

The argument does not say that prices are imaginary or that exchanging goods is merely a misunderstanding. It says that a real social process can obscure its own construction. People encounter its results as constraints even when no single person chose the entire arrangement. The market value of an object can affect a livelihood as forcefully as a physical obstacle, while remaining dependent on relationships that could have been organized otherwise.

The hypothetical factory sale makes this visible. A transfer of ownership can leave productive equipment untouched and change the conditions under which it is used. A closure can leave workers and machines capable of cooperating while removing the authorization, funding, or expected return that previously brought them together. Physical possibility and socially effective access have parted company.

To understand how that division arises, we need histories of access. English enclosure is one example, provided it is treated as a long and varied process. The National Archives describes multiple forms of enclosure, including the conversion of commons and open fields into newly defined holdings. Parliamentary acts became especially significant in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. Enclosure awards and maps recorded rights, boundaries, roads, and maintenance obligations.[2]

A boundary in such a record is not just a line on paper. It can alter who may cultivate, graze animals, gather resources, or pass through a place. Its effects depend on enforcement and on the claims that survive the reorganization. The physical land remains, but the means by which people can reproduce their lives from it change.

The exact consequences cannot be assumed identical everywhere. Holdings, compensation, local rights, and alternative livelihoods differed. Parliamentary history itself notes disagreement about how far enclosure directly drove agricultural laborers into towns.[3] We do not need one universal migration story to recognize that changing property rights can change material dependence.

If a household loses a practical means of subsistence, obtaining an income can become more urgent. If another acquires a stronger claim over productive land, it may gain a return and greater bargaining power. These are relational changes. The productive capacity of the land and the distribution of access to it must both be investigated.

Technology can reinforce such changes rather than dissolve them. Eli Whitney's 1794 cotton-gin patent concerned a machine for separating cotton fiber from seed. The labor saved in processing did not liberate the enslaved people forced to cultivate and harvest the crop. Easier processing helped make an expansion of cotton production profitable within an existing system of slavery.[4]

The general mechanism is straightforward. Lowering the cost of one stage can increase demand for complementary stages. A machine that reduces labor per unit in processing can coexist with an expansion of coerced labor in cultivation when total production grows. There is no mechanical law requiring labor-saving invention to produce freedom.

The machine was not the whole cause. Textile demand, land seizure, plantation finance, law, violence, and the domestic trade in enslaved people participated in the expansion. Enslaved people resisted the conditions imposed on them in many ways; they were never passive components of a smoothly operating apparatus.[5] An account that attributes the entire history to a device would erase both the institutions responsible and the people subjected to them.

The example also qualifies a tempting economic claim. Cheap or coerced labor can reduce incentives for some labor-saving investments. It does not prevent every kind of technical change. Owners may adopt a device that increases the profitability of coercion while resisting changes that would improve the autonomy of the people doing the work. The incentive belongs to a particular position within a relationship.

The politics of technology begins there. Asking whether an invention raises productivity is different from asking who acquires power through its use. The two can be connected, but the connection must be traced. It cannot be supplied by the reassuring word progress.

Protection presents another relationship that appears benign or coercive depending on whose position we examine. A comparison between feudal obligations and a labor union can initially seem illuminating. The comparison captures one limited idea: people may accept obligations in return for security and collective support. As a classification, however, it fails. Hereditary lordship and collective worker bargaining are different arrangements of authority, dependence, and refusal.

Rather than forcing them into one category, we can ask common questions. Who defines the obligation? Who can withdraw? Can a weaker party enforce a promise against a stronger one? Does the protector also possess the capacity to create the danger from which protection is offered? What happens when the service is inadequate?

These questions prevent a useful function from becoming a universal justification. An institution may defend a territory and extract more than its service warrants. It may protect some people while threatening others. It may continue to be supported because available alternatives are worse. Persistence does not establish consent without constraint, and dependence does not establish that resistance is impossible.

Money makes many social claims portable and repeatable. A payment can settle an obligation between people who do not otherwise share a lasting personal relationship. Accounts allow claims to survive beyond a face-to-face exchange. Institutions determine which promises count, how they are transferred, and what happens when settlement fails.

Taxation can create one source of demand for a means of payment. If an authority requires a levy to be settled in a particular unit, people subject to the levy have a reason to obtain that unit. This mechanism does not prove that every monetary system began in taxation. Trade, credit, accounting, and different forms of issuance have histories that cannot be collapsed into one origin story.

Modern bank deposits make the point concrete. As the Bank of England explains, commercial-bank lending can create deposits: the loan appears as an asset of the bank and the corresponding deposit as a liability. Banks are constrained by profitability, regulation, payment obligations, and the monetary setting; they do not create unlimited purchasing power free of consequences.[6]

Money is therefore reproduced through organized balance-sheet operations as well as through the manufacture of physical currency. Its effectiveness depends on a network of expectations and settlement arrangements. Yet the objects and services it commands still have to be produced. Creating a monetary claim does not by itself create the house, meal, or machine to which the claim may be directed.

Maintaining coin quality and managing interest rates can appear to perform a similar role. Both can affect confidence and the organization of claims, but the instruments are not equivalent. A coin's metallic content, a bank deposit, and a policy rate belong to different mechanisms and monetary regimes. A useful analogy identifies the shared problem of dependable settlement while preserving those differences.

Likewise, calling capitalism usury compresses too much. Interest, rent, profit, credit creation, and unequal bargaining are related subjects, not interchangeable names. Credit can organize a useful investment or sustain extraction; the terms, purposes, risks, and powers involved matter. A moral conclusion gains force from that distinction rather than losing it.

We can now follow one ordinary operation through the book's proposed theoretical synthesis. Imagine a worker receiving a wage payment from the factory with which we began. The example is illustrative. The worker uses part of the payment for food and housing, meets obligations, and returns for the next period of work. The employer records an expense and expects continued labor. Other institutions recognize the payment and the claims it settles.

From a Marxian perspective, we ask why the worker sells labor-power, who owns the means of production, and how the product and surplus are appropriated. The wage supports the worker's livelihood while helping reproduce a relation in which access to livelihood depends on selling the capacity to work. Marx develops this connection between production and the reproduction of the social relation itself.[7]

The point does not require imagining that a worker receives only enough to remain biologically alive. Living standards and expectations have histories, and workers can struggle over them. Nor does it require that every employer consciously plan the reproduction of a class system. Recurrent actions taken for local reasons can sustain a wider relation.

Now consider the payment as communication. It is understood as a wage because of an employment relation, an amount due, a period of work, and recognized procedures. The same numerical transfer could mean a gift, a refund, or an error under other circumstances. Its social significance is not contained in the digits alone.

Luhmann's *Social Systems* treats communication as the basic operation through which social systems reproduce themselves. His account distinguishes information, utterance, and understanding; understanding is not necessarily agreement. People and their consciousness are indispensable conditions but are not simply the component units of the social system in his technical vocabulary.[8]

This perspective directs attention to what makes another operation possible. A payment recognized as settling a wage can lead to an acknowledgment, a new obligation, a dispute, or another period of employment. A misunderstood or rejected payment can produce a different sequence. The continuity depends partly on distinctions that participants can use and institutions can stabilize.

A third perspective asks about regulation. Cybernetics studies relationships among variation, feedback, and control. Ashby's analysis emphasizes that successful regulation must be adequate to the range of disturbances a system faces.[9] In our example, the organization may respond to labor shortages, delayed payments, rising costs, or declining orders. Its routines try to keep selected conditions within a workable range.

The question immediately becomes political: which conditions, selected by whom? Management may treat the wage bill as a variable to minimize, while workers treat reliable income as a condition of life. A feedback mechanism can stabilize one party's objective by making another party's life less stable. Regulation is not synonymous with a shared good.

The three descriptions illuminate different relations within the same event. Livelihood and ownership explain material dependence and appropriation. Communication explains how an operation has a recognizable meaning and connects to further operations. Feedback explains how responses can maintain or change a pattern. Their conjunction is this book's proposal; it is not an assertion that Marx, Luhmann, and Ashby were writing the same theory.

They resist easy combination. Marx's analysis foregrounds historical conflict and class relations. Luhmann's theoretical units and system boundaries are different. A cybernetic description can be applied to many organizations without supplying a judgment about exploitation. To place the approaches in conversation, we must preserve the questions one approach makes easier to overlook.

For example, a communication-centered account can recognize threats as communications, but this book must also follow what happens to bodies when a threat is carried out. Hunger and confinement are not exhausted by their meanings. Conversely, a resource account cannot explain why physically similar transfers create different obligations unless it attends to social interpretation. Both material dependence and meaningful distinctions belong in the causal chain.

Biological autopoiesis provides another comparison, but not a shortcut. A cell's network produces components that maintain its organization. A firm's recurring decisions and payments help sustain an organization, but the firm does not biologically produce the people who work within it. Households, education, public systems, and other firms participate in its continuation. Calling both self-producing does not make their boundaries or mechanisms identical.

The comparison is productive when it prompts a precise question: what operations must recur for this organization to continue as this kind of organization? It becomes misleading when it gives an institution the appearance of a natural organism whose survival overrides the claims of its participants. People can have reasons to dismantle or transform an institution that reproduces itself effectively.

A social world also contains overlapping forms of organization. A person participates in a household, workplace, legal order, friendship network, and perhaps a profession or political association. These are not neatly nested boxes with one command at the top. Different institutions can make incompatible demands on the same person.

This is another point where hierarchy and functional differentiation must be distinguished. Luhmann's differentiation of social systems does not describe society as one enlarged company.[8] The analytical question is how operations connect across different domains, not simply who occupies the highest rung of one universal ladder.

The overlaps can create room for change. A worker's claim may be weak inside a firm and stronger through law or collective organization. A scientific finding may challenge an industry's preferred account of harm. A public institution may provide a capability that private investment neglects. No single system fully contains all the conditions on which it depends.

Conformity helps many arrangements continue. Shared expectations reduce the need to renegotiate every action. A promise is easier to use when people generally honor it; a safety procedure works better when departures are visible. Groups may reward compliance and sanction violations.

The same processes can suppress correction. A person who identifies a damaging practice may be treated as disloyal because the practice supports existing authority. If everyone receives information through the same channel and fears the same penalty, agreement may reflect dependence rather than independent confirmation. Stability of belief is not the same as accuracy.

Nor should every cooperative act be reduced to fear. People care for one another, identify with shared projects, and regard some obligations as worth honoring. These motives are part of the world to be explained. Their embodiment in brains, speech, habits, and institutions makes them material in the relevant sense without making them trivial.

This is why materialism cannot simply dismiss ideology as false and therefore unreal. An ideology can misrepresent some relationships and accurately describe others. It can help coordinate action, justify authority, or organize a challenge to authority. Even a false account can have real effects when people act through it. Calling it unreal would leave those effects unexplained.

A stronger analysis asks how an idea is learned, which experiences make it plausible, what institutions circulate it, whose position it supports, and how people respond when events contradict it. Material conditions shape the field in which beliefs develop, but people also use beliefs to interpret and alter those conditions. Causation travels through the relationship.

We should therefore treat economic explanation as a disciplined preference rather than an automatic victory. Before attributing behavior to a national character or an individual's inexplicable defect, investigate livelihood, ownership, risk, and available alternatives. But do not save a failed explanation by inventing an invisible payoff that makes every possible action economically inevitable. A hypothesis must risk being wrong.

The illustrative wage cycle offers practical tests. If missed payments make workers unable to return, a livelihood dependency is visible. If a transfer fails because its status is disputed despite available funds, meaning and authority matter. If management responds to a temporary shortage in a way that worsens the longer problem, the feedback design matters. The synthesis earns its place when such distinctions improve explanation.

It can also reveal why a functioning system becomes difficult to change. The wage relation supports households; the households support workers; the workers sustain production; production supports payments and claims. A proposed transformation can threaten people who dislike the existing arrangement but depend on its next successful cycle. Their reluctance is not proof that the arrangement is just. It is evidence of how deeply its reproduction has entered ordinary life.

The challenge is to change relations while preserving or improving the capabilities people need. That may involve collective bargaining, public provision, new ownership forms, or changes in law and investment. Each must be assessed through its actual operation. A different name on the institution does not guarantee a different pattern of power.

The factory sale at the opening now has a fuller meaning. The machine's physical capacities were continuous across the transaction. Its economic powers depended on a continuing field of recognition, enforcement, payment, and access. Those powers appeared to reside in the object because the relationships sustaining them usually worked without needing to be explained.

Making the relationships visible does not instantly dissolve them. It does, however, change the question from “What does the machine naturally demand?” to “What arrangement are we reproducing through its use?” That question can expose a contradiction between providing a useful service and maintaining the value of claims built around it.

A house makes the contradiction especially familiar. Its inhabitants need a roof. Its owner may need an asset. Its lender needs repayment. These needs can be connected without being aligned. The next chapter begins at that front door.

